{
  "id": "afa4c0b7-071b-46c3-a33b-012844729acc",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "GlobalStore Usage in Patient Chart",
  "updatedAt": 1793548629056,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "JgVaPrpddntn3g09",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "If `createGlobalStore` in the OpenMRS frontend framework (openmrs-esm-core) changed its behaviour, which code in the patient chart app (openmrs-esm-patient-chart) uses it and could be affected?",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "`createGlobalStore` is the OpenMRS frontend's factory for a **named global store** — a small shared state container (built on Zustand) that several microfrontends can read and write. The patient chart app calls it in exactly **three** places, and one of them is a hand-written mock rather than the real function. Everything else in the app is affected indirectly, because it reads those same stores through React hooks.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## What the function does today",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The real implementation lives in the framework repo `openmrs-esm-core` [1]. Two behaviours matter for the patient chart:",
          "citations": [
            1
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "- **A name is created only once.** It keeps a registry `availableStores` [2]. If a store with that name already exists and is *active*, it logs an error and returns the existing store instead of the new one [3].\n- **An inactive store is re-activated and reset.** If the name exists but is inactive (which is how `getGlobalStore` parks a placeholder), its state is overwritten with the `initialState` you passed [4]. `initialState` is dropped entirely when the store already exists.",
          "citations": [
            2,
            3,
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The third parameter, `storageType` (`'none'` or `'sessionStorage'`) [5], is one the patient chart never uses.",
          "citations": [
            5
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## The three direct call sites in the patient chart",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "| Location | Store name | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| `orderBasketStore` [6] | `order-basket` | Holds in-progress orders per patient, keyed by grouping |\n| `patientChartStore` [7] | `patient-chart-global-store` | Holds the chart's patient and visit context |\n| `store` in the Angular form wrapper [8] | `ampath-form-state` | Tracks the form's loading/submitting state per form UUID |",
          "citations": [
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The first two sit in `esm-patient-common-lib`, which every other patient-chart package depends on, so they are the ones with real blast radius.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "### 1. The order basket (`order-basket`)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The store object is created at module load [6], and the exported `orderBasketStore` is then used in three ways:",
          "citations": [
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "- **React hook `useOrderBasket`** wraps it with the framework's `useStoreWithActions` and defines the actions that write to it [9].\n- **Direct reads/writes outside React**: `clearOrders` reads and replaces state with `getState()` / `setState()` [10], and the order-posting code reads the basket to build the POST payload [11] and [12].\n- **A test-only reset helper** `_resetOrderBasketStore` that pushes `initialState` back in [13].",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10,
            11,
            12,
            13
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "One hop outward, `useOrderBasket` is called by the order basket workspace [14], the exported-lab-results workspace [15] and the order-basket action button that shows the count on the chart header [16]. Order-posting tests use the store directly [17].",
          "citations": [
            14,
            15,
            16,
            17
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "### 2. The patient chart store (`patient-chart-global-store`)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Created once at module scope [7], then exposed two ways: the hook `usePatientChartStore` [18] and the plain setter `setPatientChartWorkspaceGroupVisitUuid` [19]. This is the widest-reaching use — roughly twenty non-test files across `esm-patient-chart-app`, `esm-patient-banner-app`, `esm-patient-vitals-app` and `esm-patient-immunizations-app` consume the hook, including the visit tag and past-visit tag banner extensions, the visit context header and switcher, the current-visit summary, the encounter and visits tables, `useDeleteVisit` [20], the visit form workspace [21], and the shared `useLaunchWorkspaceRequiringVisit` helper [22]. The chart resource that owns the store also writes the workspace-group visit UUID back [23].",
          "citations": [
            7,
            18,
            19,
            20,
            21,
            22,
            23
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "### 3. The Angular form wrapper (`ampath-form-state`) — not really affected",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "This call [8] subscribes to the store to mirror form state into the component [24]. But in the Angular test setup it is replaced by a **local stub** of `createGlobalStore` with its own closure over state and subscribers, which has none of the registry, dedup or session-storage behaviour [25]. So a behaviour change would not show up there in Angular tests, and the name `ampath-form-state` is used nowhere else in the repo.",
          "citations": [
            8,
            24,
            25
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## How a behaviour change would propagate",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Because a store name is a global key, a change to the function's collision or activation rules ripples through the registry rather than through a single caller:",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- If creation becomes per-call (each call returns a *new* store rather than the existing one), the order basket and the patient chart store would silently stop being shared — every component would read an empty or stale copy, with no error. That is exactly the failure mode the framework's own duplicate-module warning describes [26].\n- If the \"inactive store\" reset branch changes [4], anyone who calls `getGlobalStore` with one of these two names before the store module is imported would get a differently-initialised store. **I found no such call in the patient chart** — grepping for `getGlobalStore` and `registerGlobalStore` in `openmrs-esm-patient-chart` returned no matches, so this path is only reachable from other apps.\n- The `storageType` default only matters if the patient chart starts passing `'sessionStorage'`; today it never does [6].",
          "citations": [
            26,
            4,
            6
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: \"createGlobalStore impact in the patient chart\"\n---\nflowchart TD\n  core[\"createGlobalStore in esm-core\"] --> reg[\"availableStores registry\"]\n  reg --> basket[\"orderBasketStore order-basket\"]\n  reg --> chart[\"patientChartStore patient-chart-global-store\"]\n  reg --> ang[\"ampath-form-state in Angular wrapper\"]\n  basket --> hook[\"useOrderBasket\"]\n  hook --> obWorkspace[\"Order basket workspace\"]\n  hook --> labWorkspace[\"Exported lab results workspace\"]\n  hook --> actionButton[\"Order basket action button\"]\n  basket --> postOrders[\"postOrders payload build\"]\n  chart --> usePCS[\"usePatientChartStore\"]\n  usePCS --> many[\"Visit, encounter, vitals, banner, immunizations components\"]\n  chart --> setter[\"setPatientChartWorkspaceGroupVisitUuid\"]\n  ang --> stub[\"Angular test stub of createGlobalStore\"]\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## What I could not verify",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "- I only inspected the patient chart app as asked. Other picked repositories (for example `openmrs-esm-core`'s own tests and storybook mocks [27], or sibling apps like dispensing and laboratory) also call this function and were out of scope.\n- The two other `createGlobalStore` definitions I saw in `openmrs-esm-core` are test/mock-only variants [28], [29]; if a behaviour change touched only `src/state.ts` they would diverge silently, but I did not trace that further.\n- The exact runtime behaviour of `useStoreWithActions` and `useStore` (in `openmrs-esm-core`) is what turns a store change into a re-render, and I did not read that code, so the precise rendering consequences are inferred, not confirmed.\n- `store.subscribe` in the Angular wrapper [24] is exercised against the stub, so any change specific to the real store's subscription or `setState` semantics would not be caught there.\n</answer_style>",
          "citations": [
            27,
            28,
            29,
            24
          ]
        }
      ],
